+[vc_row][vc_column][woodmart_text_block woodmart_css_id="64ea221f659fe" parallax_scroll="no" woodmart_inline="no" wd_hide_on_desktop="no" wd_hide_on_tablet_landscape="no" wd_hide_on_tablet="no" wd_hide_on_mobile="no" responsive_spacing="eyJwYXJhbV90eXBlIjoid29vZG1hcnRfcmVzcG9uc2l2ZV9zcGFjaW5nIiwic2VsZWN0b3JfaWQiOiI2NGVhMjIxZjY1OWZlIiwic2hvcnRjb2RlIjoid29vZG1hcnRfdGV4dF9ibG9jayIsImRhdGEiOnsidGFibGV0Ijp7fSwibW9iaWxlIjp7fX19" woodmart_empty_space=""]<p>FBX is a 3D file format used to exchange models and scene data between applications. An .fbx file can contain geometry, materials, animation, cameras and lights, making it useful for rendering, animation and game development.</p>
+<p>The result of an FBX transfer depends on the exporting and importing applications, the file version and the selected settings. Geometry may transfer successfully while materials, texture connections or animation require adjustment in the destination software.</p>
+<p>This guide explains what FBX files can store, where the format is useful, and its main compatibility limits. It also covers considerations when converting a model for 3D printing.</p>[/woodmart_text_block][woodmart_image img_align="center" rounding_size="" click_action="lightbox" img_id="14584" img_size="full" woodmart_box_shadow="no" parallax_scroll="no" woodmart_inline="no" wd_hide_on_desktop="no" wd_hide_on_tablet_landscape="no" wd_hide_on_tablet="no" wd_hide_on_mobile="no" mobile_reset_margin="no" tablet_reset_margin="no" woodmart_css_id="64ea220f80c1f" display_inline="no" responsive_spacing="eyJwYXJhbV90eXBlIjoid29vZG1hcnRfcmVzcG9uc2l2ZV9zcGFjaW5nIiwic2VsZWN0b3JfaWQiOiI2NGVhMjIwZjgwYzFmIiwic2hvcnRjb2RlIjoid29vZG1hcnRfaW1hZ2UiLCJkYXRhIjp7InRhYmxldCI6e30sIm1vYmlsZSI6e319fQ=="][woodmart_title align="left" style="underlined" color="primary" size="medium" title="CAPABILITIES AND FEATURES" woodmart_css_id="64ea222b631d2" responsive_spacing="eyJwYXJhbV90eXBlIjoid29vZG1hcnRfcmVzcG9uc2l2ZV9zcGFjaW5nIiwic2VsZWN0b3JfaWQiOiI2NGVhMjIyYjYzMWQyIiwic2hvcnRjb2RlIjoid29vZG1hcnRfdGl0bGUiLCJkYXRhIjp7InRhYmxldCI6e30sIm1vYmlsZSI6e319fQ==" woodmart_empty_space=""][woodmart_text_block woodmart_css_id="64ea2384a6288" parallax_scroll="no" woodmart_inline="no" wd_hide_on_desktop="no" wd_hide_on_tablet_landscape="no" wd_hide_on_tablet="no" wd_hide_on_mobile="no" responsive_spacing="eyJwYXJhbV90eXBlIjoid29vZG1hcnRfcmVzcG9uc2l2ZV9zcGFjaW5nIiwic2VsZWN0b3JfaWQiOiI2NGVhMjM4NGE2Mjg4Iiwic2hvcnRjb2RlIjoid29vZG1hcnRfdGV4dF9ibG9jayIsImRhdGEiOnsidGFibGV0Ijp7fSwibW9iaWxlIjp7fX19" woodmart_empty_space=""]The FBX file format boasts numerous useful features. One of its most prominent capabilities is its handling of special effects. Similar to formats like <a href="https://shustrik-maps.com/understanding-the-obj-file-format-a-simple-explanation/">OBJ</a> and DAE (Collada), FBX can manage and store special effects data for 3D models, including:
